From f25f2427470de02b3d85cf91810bef28c44c39b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivel=C3=A4?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:44:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] extensions: tell an extension the window it opened closed

windows.create answered with the one id every window call reports, so
the page an extension opened had no id of its own, and the series raised
none of the tabs and windows events it declares. Bitwarden ends a
passkey request, and lets the site fall back, when its prompt's window
is removed. A reader who closed the prompt waited out the site's timeout
instead.

A page an extension opens with windows.create is now a window of its
own, holding that one tab, under an id no other window of the session
has. windows.create answers with it, and windows.get, getCurrent from
the page itself, and getAll answer for it while the page is open, with
the type the extension asked for. After the page closes, windows.get
fails with "No window with id".

When the page's WebContents goes, however it went, every extension
listening hears tabs.onRemoved for its tab, with isWindowClosing set,
and then windows.onRemoved for the window, as Chrome raises them. A
listener that filters by window type hears about the window only if it
asked for the window's type, and hears it once. The page can close
because the application deleted it, because it called window.close(), or
because the extension called windows.remove or tabs.remove, which now
ask the page to close the way window.close() does.

windows.remove refuses the browser's own window, and tabs.remove refuses
a tab that is not a window of its own. The reader's tabs and the
browser's window stay the application's to close, and no other tabs or
windows event is raised.

OpenPage now notices an application that lets the page go while it is
handed over, rather than loading into a page that is gone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
